Let's discuss: You can be handed GPU capacity — some of it ours, some of it rented, none of it uniform — along with a set of business and security requirements, and turn it into production infrastructure the rest of the company can trust and operate. You will help us answer questions such as: Should this workload run on bare metal, a VM, or a container? How should we provision, rebuild, and recover machines we cannot walk up to? Where does owned hardware genuinely beat rented capacity, and where are we just paying for the privilege? What should customer isolation look like? Where should we standardize, and where should we preserve flexibility? How do we operate owned hardware and external compute as one coherent platform rather than two? Which parts of the infrastructure should become product capabilities rather than internal operations?
Bare metal, VM, or container: decide by GPU access, isolation, and lifetime, not by preference.
Bare metal gives direct PCIe access, full driver and firmware control, and no hypervisor tax, which matters for long multi-node training and for topology-sensitive work. The cost is slow provisioning and a node-sized blast radius.
A VM gives the strongest isolation for untrusted work, plus snapshot and clean teardown, but GPU passthrough kills most live migration, and you pay overhead and complexity in the virtualization layer. That is the layer you know from OpenStack on GPU servers and VMware, not from production KVM, so that distinction matters if they probe it.
A container is fast and portable, but it shares a kernel. That is fine for trusted internal batch, especially with device control like Slurm's cgroup device allowlist, and it is not enough by itself for untrusted customers on a shared node.
My rule of thumb: untrusted tenant gets a VM or a whole node, never a plain container on a shared GPU node. Trusted batch gets containers on bare metal. Short-lived inference gets containers. Long training gets bare metal with topology-aware placement. And on a card like the T4, with 16 GB, PCIe Gen3, no NVLink, and no MIG, you cannot pretend sharing is free. Sharing has to be explicit: time-slicing, MPS within one tenant, or whole-node lending, which is exactly the node-broker model you designed for Slurm and LSF.

Provision, rebuild, and recover machines you cannot walk up to: make rebuild the normal path, not the emergency path.
No SSH-and-fix snowflakes. A node should be: discovered, imaged from a golden image, configured from code, health-gated, and only then returned to service. Recovery is a ladder: drain, try a soft reset, reimage, and if that fails, quarantine the node with exact diagnostics for remote hands, including which FRU is suspected.
Out-of-band access is non-negotiable: BMC, Redfish, serial-over-LAN, and kernel logs over the network for cases where userspace is dead. Your ESX lesson applies here too: boot order matters, and a machine booting the wrong disk or PXE first will look like a storage or SAN problem when it is really a boot-order problem.
The gate before return is what builds trust: GPU reset between jobs, ECC baseline, a short test job, and driver and firmware versions matching the supported matrix. A node that fails a gate does not go back into the pool quietly.

Owned versus rented: compare total cost per useful GPU-hour, not sticker price.
Owned looks cheap per hour until you add power, space, spares, remote hands, depreciation, and idle time. Rented looks flexible until you add egress, availability risk, noisy neighbors, and provider terms changing under you.
Owned genuinely wins for steady, high-utilization, predictable work, especially where data stays put or where you need a specific topology or storage locality. Rented wins for bursts, experiments, new regions, and short-lived peaks.
You are just paying for the privilege when you rent your steady base load at a premium, or when you own capacity that sits idle most of the time to cover a rare peak. The practical shape is usually: own the base, rent the peak, and re-price it quarterly on utilization, cost per GPU-hour, and queue wait time, not on a one-time spreadsheet.

Customer isolation: build it in layers, and assume the claim will be attacked.
No single mechanism is enough. You need: one tenant per GPU at a time, or per node for untrusted work; GPU memory reset between tenants, because GPU memory is not reliably cleared by just ending a process; no shared MPS daemon across tenants; network segmentation so tenants cannot see each other; separate storage volumes with no cross-mounts; separate identities with no shared service accounts; and audit logs that actually show who ran what where.
Your ITAR enclave work gives you the right mindset here: hardened access, separate enclaves, and compliance-driven separation. It is not the same as proving a multi-tenant product isolation claim, and that is an honest boundary to state. The test is: if a tenant runs hostile code on a GPU, can it read another tenant's memory, escape to the host, or reach another tenant's network? If you cannot answer that with a mechanism, not a policy, the isolation claim does not hold.

Standardize versus flexibility: standardize the boring parts, keep flexibility at the workload boundary.
Standardize the OS image, the driver and firmware matrix, monitoring, logging, identity, networking, storage access, and the provisioning API. Those are where variation creates operational cost with no customer value.
Preserve flexibility where it changes performance or cost materially: GPU model, interconnect, placement constraints, scheduler choice, and container runtime. The trick is to express flexibility as a placement constraint, not as a snowflake configuration. Support a small, versioned matrix of combinations, and treat anything outside it as a time-limited exception. That is the same idea as node categories in a cluster manager: one T4 category, one image, one set of health checks, not fifty hand-built nodes.

One coherent platform across owned and rented: one control plane, honest capabilities.
Do not pretend rented capacity is identical to owned capacity. That lie breaks trust the first time a job fails for a provider-specific reason.
What should be common: one inventory, one queue, one identity system, one observability stack, one cost accounting system in GPU-hours, and one provisioning API.
What should stay visible to the placement engine, but not necessarily to the user: GPU model, interconnect, storage locality, egress cost, availability, and isolation level. Jobs should declare requirements: GPU count and memory, interconnect need, isolation level, data locality, max cost, and max wait. The platform picks the source. And failures need different handling: a provider API outage is not a node failure, and it should trigger retry or fallback, not a node quarantine.

Product capabilities versus internal operations: productize only what you would document, version, and support.
Productize when a capability is repeatable, can be exposed as an API with an SLA, and is something customers choose you for: provisioning, GPU-hour metering, isolation guarantees, placement and topology selection, checkpoint and resume, and usable observability.
Keep it internal when it is a workaround, a provider-specific hack, a manual remote-hands process, or something that would lock you into a shape you will regret. A good test is: would you charge for it and support it for a customer you have never met? If not, keep it internal until you have a second real use case that forces a clean abstraction. Productizing too early freezes a bad interface.

